    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65014",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems",
      "problem": "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=10$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$",
      "worked_solution": "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only"
            },
