    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-337919",
      "knowledge_point_id": "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs",
      "problem": "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0$ is {{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=2x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2x,$ which is different from zero.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "choices": [
